--TEST--
`Core\Request::cookie` is unavailable exactly when the `Cookie` field it parses is, because it reads that field rather than a set of its own -- `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`, `rule:errors/cookie-name-bytes`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, asked of the three members that answer off one field. `cookie` is
// a *reading* of the `Cookie` header: there is no second place a request's
// cookies are kept, so nothing can hold a set of them that disagrees with the
// headers the request arrived with -- and the way that shows from a program is
// that all three become available and unavailable together.
uint $together = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::header("cookie");
} catch (LogicError $one) {
    $together = $together + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::headers();
} catch (LogicError $all) {
    $together = $together + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::cookie("session");
} catch (LogicError $parsed) {
    $together = $together + 1;
}
echo "the field and its reading refused together: ", $together, " of 3\n";

// The reading names itself and not the field it went through, so a handler
// standing on both learns which question it asked.
try {
    Core\Request::cookie("session");
} catch (LogicError $why) {
    echo $why->message, "\n";
}

// A throw rather than a fatal, so the program is still here to ask again.
echo "still running\n";
--EXPECT--
the field and its reading refused together: 3 of 3
Core\Request::cookie(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
still running
